# RFC 9755: on a connection that has enabled UTF8=ACCEPT, mailbox names
# are UTF-8, in commands and in responses alike. On a connection that
# has not, they are mUTF-7. The first connection here checks the mUTF-7
# side, the second the UTF-8 side.
depends twologins
send 1
a create &CQkJJgk+CTkJMAkj-
receive 1
a OK done
send 1
b list "" &CQkJJgk+CTkJMAkj-
receive 2
* LIST (\hasnochildren) "/" &CQkJJgk+CTkJMAkj-
b OK done
send 1
c status &CQkJJgk+CTkJMAkj- (messages)
receive 2
* STATUS &CQkJJgk+CTkJMAkj- (MESSAGES 0)
c OK done
# The UTF-8 side sends one name as a quoted string and one as a
# literal; RFC 9755 permits both. The server replies with a literal
# either way, so the expected responses are the same.
use second
send 1
d enable utf8=accept
receive 2
* ENABLED UTF8=ACCEPT
d OK done
send 1
e list "" "उदाहरण"
receive 3
* LIST (\hasnochildren) "/" {18}
उदाहरण
e OK done
send 2
f status {18+}
उदाहरण (messages)
receive 3
* STATUS {18}
उदाहरण (MESSAGES 0)
f OK done
end
